    "long_instructions": "## Who decides what gets filtered out?\r\n\r\n#### **Answer the ethics question in the box below**.\r\n#### If you get stuck, open “Check your thinking” to see examples and prompts that may help you reflect.\r\n\r\n<div style=\"display:flex; gap:18px; align-items:flex-start; margin:18px auto; max-width:85%;\">\r\n\r\n  <!-- Image (1/4 width) -->\r\n  <div style=\"flex:1;\">\r\n    <img\r\n      src=\"https://images.code.org/8ff79b9c90ea606077483bb1f2b8b980-image-1770220703434.png\"\r\n      alt=\"\"\r\n      style=\"width:100%; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.15);\"\r\n    />\r\n  </div>\r\n\r\n  <!-- Text box (3/4 width) -->\r\n  <div style=\"flex:3; background-color:#f3f0ff; border-radius:10px; padding:16px; box-sizing:border-box;\">\r\n    <p style=\"font-size:14pt; margin-top:0; line-height:140%;\">\r\n      <strong>Ethics question:</strong> If an algorithm filters out certain viewpoints “to keep people engaged,” is that helping users… or controlling them?\r\n    </p>\r\n    <p style=\"font-size:13pt; margin-bottom:0; line-height:140%;\">\r\n      <i>Consider who benefits from the filter rules: the user, the platform, advertisers, or someone else.</i>\r\n    </p>\r\n  </div>\r\n\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<details style=\"background-color:#ececec; padding:10px; margin-bottom:10px; border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:8px;\"> <summary style=\"font-size:1.4em; font-weight:bold;\">💡 Check your thinking</summary> <div style=\"margin-top:12px; margin-left:14px; margin-right:14px; margin-bottom:10px; font-size:13pt; line-height:145%;\"> <p style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Filters aren’t neutral.</strong> Even when they’re “automatic,” humans choose goals and tradeoffs—like prioritizing engagement, safety, personalization, or profit.</p> <ul> <li style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Platform designers</strong> decide what the algorithm optimizes for.</li> <li style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Policy teams</strong> decide what content is allowed or removed.</li> <li style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Users</strong> sometimes choose filters—when the design makes them visible.</li> </ul> <p style=\"font-size:13pt;\"><strong>Transparency matters</strong> because it helps users understand what they’re not seeing and make informed decisions.</p> </div> </details>\r\n\r\n",
